 "public_summary": "TESS shows a 1.5-hour, flat-bottomed 681 ppm dip repeating every 2.57 days on this 0.7 solar-radius K dwarf, with 63 transits and no odd/even or secondary eclipses. Centroiding places the dip on the target and rules out the one nearby star that could mimic it, and the 2.2 Earth-radius size matches the stellar density. The star is noticeably variable, which explains the modest phase-curve wobble but does not produce the transit shape or period relation expected for spots, so a planet is the best explanation pending radial-velocity confirmation.",
